course: money
lesson: 38
title: Crypto and the stock market, simply
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The stock market is a place where people buy and sell shares, which are little bits of real companies; if the company does well, its share price tends to rise, and if it does badly, it falls.
explain: Picture buying 1 share for £10: if it goes to £12 you are £2 up, and if it drops to £8 you are £2 down; nobody knows for sure which way it will go, not even for a famous company.
explain: Crypto is digital money not run by a government or a bank; its price jumps up and down very fast, as is widely reported, so some people make money and many lose it, like Theo's cousin whose £100 became £50 in a week.
explain: Grown-ups strongly disagree about crypto: some call it the future of money, others call it a bubble or mostly guessing; nobody knows for sure.
explain: The safe rules are simple: anything that promises huge, guaranteed profit is a scam; spreading money across many companies lowers risk; and online videos show the few winners, not the many losers.
fact: The stock market is where shares are bought and sold; a share price is the cost of one share.
fact: Buying a share at £10 and selling at £12 gains £2; if it falls to £8, the loss is £2.
fact: Crypto is digital money not run by a government or a bank; volatile means changing very fast; £100 of crypto falling to £50 is a £50 loss.
fact: Many crypto scams promise huge returns: turning £10 into £1,000 in a month means 100 times more, and no real investment can promise that.
fact: Bitcoin is the most famous cryptocurrency; its price has been reported to jump up and down a lot, for example from £20,000 to £50,000, which is up £30,000, then down to £30,000, which is down £20,000.
fact: Speculation is buying because you hope the price will go up; a bubble is when prices rise far above real value and then burst.
fact: People strongly disagree about crypto: some see new money for the world, others mostly speculation; nobody knows for sure who is right.
fact: Many pensions, money saved for old age, are invested in shares; if a pension owns 1,000 shares and each falls by 30p, it is worth £300 less that day.
fact: Over many years share markets have usually gone up, but not always, and in some years they have lost a fifth of their value or more.
fact: To diversify is to spread your money around; an index fund invests in many companies at once, so £100 spread over 100 companies puts £1 in each, and one company going bust loses only £1.
fact: No share is guaranteed to go up; even big famous companies can fall.
fact: Shares are not quite gambling, because you own a real piece of a real business; but treating shares like a bet is dangerous.
fact: Hype is lots of excitement; a meme stock is a share that becomes popular online; if 1 person gains £1,000 and 10 people lose £100 each, the losers lost £1,000 in total.
exercise: What is the stock market? | A place where people buy and sell bits of companies | A market where farmers buy and sell cows, sheep and pigs | A shop that sells soup stock cubes | A bank that keeps everyone's savings
exercise: What is a share? | A small piece of a company | A loan to a company's workers | A ticket for free products | A whole company bought by one person
exercise: Jake buys 1 share for £10 and it rises to £12. How much is he up? | £2 | £12 | £22 | £10
exercise: Theo's cousin bought £100 of crypto and it's now worth £50. How much did he lose? | £50 | £100 | £150 | £25
exercise: What is crypto? | Digital money not run by a government or a bank | Coins buried by pirates long ago | Money that is printed by the Bank of England in London | A secret bank account for children
exercise: An ad says "Invest £10, get £1,000 in a month!" How many times more is that? | 100 times | 10 times | 1,000 times | 990 times
exercise: Why does Dad say that ad is a scam? | No real investment can promise such huge, guaranteed profit | Crypto ads are always true, but this one is too small | Investing £10 is against the law for adults | The ad didn't use enough capital letters
exercise: Bitcoin went from £20,000 to £50,000, then down to £30,000. How much did it fall from its high? | £20,000 | £30,000 | £10,000 | £50,000
exercise: Kai's uncle says crypto is the future and his aunt says it's a bubble. Who is right? | Nobody knows for sure; people strongly disagree | The uncle, because everyone in the world agrees with him | The aunt, because everyone agrees | Neither, because crypto doesn't exist
exercise: What is a bubble? | When prices rise far above real value and then burst | When prices stay the same for many years | A safe investment that always grows | A kind of cryptocurrency for children
exercise: Grandad's pension owns 1,000 shares, and each share falls by 30p in a day. How much less is it worth that day? | £300 | £30 | £3,000 | £3
exercise: Grace's mum puts £100 into an index fund with 100 companies. How much is in each company? | £1 | £100 | £10 | 10p
exercise: Why is an index fund less risky? | One bad company can't wipe out all your money | Index funds are guaranteed to go up every day | The government pays you back if you lose | Index funds only buy companies that never fall
exercise: Leo's mate says, "Nike shares will never drop." What did Leo's dad say? | Everything can drop, son | He's right, famous shares never fall | Only small companies' shares go up | Nike shares are free for teenagers
exercise: Ruby asks, "Is the stock market gambling?" What did Dad say? | Kind of, but you own real bits of real companies | Yes, exactly like a scratchcard you buy at the corner shop | No, shares can never lose any money | Yes, and it's illegal for adults
exercise: A TikTok teen got £1,000 from meme stocks, while 10 others lost £100 each. How much did the 10 lose in total? | £1,000 | £100 | £10,000 | £110
test: Theo's cousin goes, "Bought crypto, gonna be rich, no cap!" A week later: "It's crashed, I'm down half." ("No cap" means for real.) What does this show? | Crypto is very volatile: prices can fall fast | Crypto always doubles in value in a week, so it's safe | Crypto is the safest place for savings | He lost nothing because crypto can't fall
test: An ad on your feed screams, "Invest £50, get £5,000 by Friday, guaranteed!" What's the smart move? | Ignore it, because huge guaranteed profits are a scam | Tell your parents to send £50 straight away | Share it with friends so they can get rich too | Send £5,000 to be sure you win more
test: Grandad grumbles, "Market fell today — there goes my pension!" Should he panic? | Over many years markets have usually risen, though not always | Yes, his pension is now worth £0 for ever | Yes, markets never recover after a fall | No, because pensions are never in shares
test: A Gen Z cousin says, "Just put all your money in one stock, it's a vibe." ("A vibe" means it feels good.) What's the risk? | If that one company falls, you could lose a lot | There's no risk at all, as one stock is much safer than many | You'd get guaranteed profit from one stock | Shares can only rise when you own just one
test: Jake buys a share for £10. It drops to £8 — wait, £7. How much is he down? | £3 | £2 | £17 | £7
test: What would happen if a famous company had a terrible year? | Its share price would probably fall | Its share price would definitely double | Its shareholders would get free products | Nothing, famous companies' shares never move
test: Kai's aunt, from Edinburgh, says, "Crypto's a bubble, ken, it'll burst." ("Ken" means "you know".) His uncle says it's the future. Mum says, "Nobody knows for sure, love." Which is the honest answer? | Mum's: grown-ups disagree and nobody can be sure | The aunt's: crypto will burst on a set date | The uncle's: crypto is certain to win | Kai's: he should buy crypto to find out
test: In a cartoon, a boy buys one share and becomes boss of the whole company. What's wrong? | One share is a tiny piece; the company has lots of owners | Nothing, one share makes you the boss | Shares are only for buying sweets | Bosses are chosen by a coin flip each morning
test: A teenager on TikTok says, "I got rich off meme stocks, easy money." His sister says, "Loads lost money too." Why do we hear mostly about winners? | Videos show the winners, not the many who lost | Nobody ever loses money on meme stocks if they hold on long enough | The government bans videos about losses | Losers are always paid back by TikTok
test: Bitcoin hit a record high of £50,000, as reported, then fell to £30,000. A man bought 2 coins at the top — no wait, just 1. What is it worth now? | £30,000 | £60,000 | £50,000 | £20,000
test: Ruby's mate says, "Shares and the lottery are the same, innit." What's the difference? | A share is a piece of a real business; a lottery ticket is only a bet on chance | Both always lose all your money | Lottery tickets are pieces of companies | Shares can never go up or down
exam: It is given that a share purchased at £10 is subsequently valued at £12. Determine the gain per share. | £2 | £12 | £22 | £10
exam: Ascertain the reduction in value of a holding of one thousand shares consequent upon a fall of thirty pence in the price of each. | £300 | £30 | £3,000 | £3
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an advertisement promising a hundredfold return within one month? | It bears the characteristics of a fraudulent scheme | It describes a conventional and secure investment of a prudent kind | It is guaranteed by the central bank | It is the established practice of index funds
exam: Identify the principal advantage of diversification across many companies. | The limitation of loss arising from the failure of any single company | The guarantee of profit in every year without exception | The exemption of the investor from all taxation | The conversion of shares into cryptocurrency
